Installing HemeLB
=================

Quick install (macOS or Debian/Ubuntu Linux)
--------------------------------------------

    git clone --branch feat/scalability-input-improvements https://github.com/lepotatoguy/hemelb.git
    cd hemelb
    Scripts/install_hemelb.sh

This builds HemeLB into ~/.local/hemelb (programs hemelb, hemelb-confcheck
and hemelb-tests) and installs the geometry tool (hlb-gmy-gui, hlb-gmy-cli)
and the Python tools into a conda environment called gmy-tool. Run
"Scripts/install_hemelb.sh --help" for the options. Full guide:
doc/user/install.md. First run: doc/user/getting-started.md.


Building by hand
----------------

    cmake -S . -B build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
          -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=$HOME/.local/hemelb
    cmake --build build -j 4

The top-level CMakeLists.txt is the "super build": it builds any missing
dependency, then HemeLB, and installs everything into CMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX.
One configure pass is enough. Choose per dependency with
-DDEPS_<NAME>=Auto|System|Build (Auto: use the system copy if found,
otherwise build it).

Other entry points:
  Code/          HemeLB only; every dependency must already be installed.
  dependencies/  Only the dependencies, for repeated development builds.

Build options (lattice, collision kernel, boundary conditions, logging):
doc/user/CMakeOptions.md. Build and run details: doc/user/main-application.md.

The geometry tool on its own, with exactly the tested environment:

    conda create -n gmy-tool --file geometry-tool/conda-lock/osx-64.txt
          (linux-64.txt on Linux; on Apple Silicon first run
           export CONDA_SUBDIR=osx-64)
    conda activate gmy-tool
    pip install --no-deps --no-build-isolation ./python-tools
    pip install './geometry-tool[gui]'

See doc/user/geometry-tool.md.


Versions
--------

Pinned: fixed exactly in this repository, so they cannot drift.
Tested: the exact versions this branch was built and tested with.

1. Libraries the super build downloads when missing (pinned by URL in
   dependencies/<Name>/build.cmake):

     Boost 1.77.0 (headers)   ParMETIS 4.0.2    TinyXML2 9.0.0
     CTemplate 2.4            zlib 1.2.6        Catch2 2.13.9 (tests)
     HDF5 1.8.15 (RBC only)   VTK 9.1.0 (RBC only)
     MPWide 1.1 (multiscale only)

   The minimum versions accepted from your system are Boost 1.77, HDF5 1.8
   and VTK 9.0; ParMETIS 4, TinyXML2 and Catch2 2 (catch2/catch.hpp) are
   the major versions the code is written for. To use exactly the versions
   above rather than your system's, configure with -DDEPS_<NAME>=Build, for
   example -DDEPS_BOOST=Build -DDEPS_PARMETIS=Build -DDEPS_tinyxml2=Build.

2. Compiler, CMake and MPI come from your system (Homebrew or apt). These
   cannot be pinned by the repository. The following historical runs used
   fix/hemelb-improvements, before this branch moved to TinyXML2 and XML6.
   Current CPU build/model verification is recorded in
   doc/dev/comparison-and-roadmap.md; these older rows do not establish that
   the feature branch passed the same remote CI jobs.
   The exact versions tested in those historical runs were:

   macOS 27, Apple Silicon (local, 2026-09-27/28)
     AppleClang 21.0.0, CMake 4.3.2, Open MPI 5.0.9, Boost 1.90.0,
     CTemplate 2.4, METIS 5.1.0 (Homebrew); bundled ParMETIS 4.0.2 and
     TinyXML 2.6.2 in a separate manual build
   macOS 15.7, Intel x86_64 (CI, install script)
     AppleClang 17.0.0, Open MPI 5.0.10, Boost 1.92.0, CTemplate 2.4,
     METIS 5.1.0 (Homebrew)
   macOS 14, Apple Silicon (CI, install script)
     AppleClang 15.0.0, Open MPI 5.0.10, Boost 1.92.0, CTemplate 2.4,
     METIS 5.1.0 (Homebrew)
   Ubuntu 24.04 x86_64 (CI and Docker, install script)
     GCC 13.3.0, CMake 3.28.3, Open MPI 4.1.6, Boost 1.83.0,
     ParMETIS 4.0.3, METIS 5.1.0, TinyXML 2.6.2, CTemplate 2.4 (apt)
   Ubuntu 22.04 x86_64 (CI, main application builds)
     GCC 11.4.0, 12.3.0 and 13.4.0; Open MPI 4.1.2, ParMETIS 4.0.3,
     METIS 5.1.0, TinyXML 2.6.2, CTemplate 2.4, HDF5 1.10.7 (apt);
     Boost 1.77.0, VTK and Catch2 from the bundled builds

   Requirements: a C++20 compiler (GCC 11 or newer, or AppleClang), CMake
   3.13 or newer, MPI 3.0 or newer (the code uses MPI-3 calls).

3. Geometry tool and Python tools: the conda environment.

   geometry-tool/conda-lock/osx-64.txt and linux-64.txt pin every package
   (220 and 266 packages) to an exact build; the install script uses them.
   geometry-tool/conda-environment.yml pins the main packages exactly:

     Python 3.11.8        VMTK 1.5.0          VTK 9.2.6
     CGAL 5.6.1           Boost headers 1.82.0  NumPy 1.26.4
     Cython 0.29.37       wxPython 4.2.1      python.app 1.4 (macOS)
     pybind11 3.1.0       scikit-build 0.19.0 CMake 3.28.3
     Ninja 1.13.2         setuptools 84.0.0   wheel 0.48.0
     PyYAML 6.0.3         pytest 9.1.1        zlib 1.3.2   pip 26.2.1

   geometry-tool/pyproject.toml pins what pip uses to build the tool:
   setuptools 75.3.0, wheel 0.45.1, scikit-build 0.19.0, pybind11 2.13.6,
   cmake 3.28.3 (these support Python 3.8 to 3.11).

   The geometry tool supports Python 3.8 to 3.11 only, because VMTK 1.5 is
   only built for those. VMTK is only on conda-forge, not PyPI. CGAL must be
   5.x: CGAL 6 removed a header the tool uses.

   The Python tools (python-tools/) are tested on Python 3.8 to 3.13 with
   Cython 0.29 and 3.x, so their build requirements are ranges, not pins;
   in the conda environment they build against the pinned versions above.


Platforms
---------

Tested with the install script: macOS 14 on Apple Silicon (CI), macOS 15 on
Intel (CI), macOS 27 on Apple Silicon (locally), and Ubuntu 24.04 x86_64 (CI
and Docker). Apple
Silicon Macs run the geometry tool environment under Rosetta 2, because
VMTK 1.5 exists only for x86_64; the script sets this up.

Not tested: Debian itself,
the install script on Ubuntu releases before 24.04 (their default compilers
may be too old for C++20; the main application itself is tested on 22.04), Linux on ARM (no VMTK build exists, so the geometry tool cannot be
installed there), and distributions without apt (install the packages above
yourself, then run the script with --no-system-deps).
